---
<LearnModule slug="inverse-problems/svd-noise-amplification" description="Interactive singular value decomposition of a blur operator: Picard plot, truncated SVD, Tikhonov filtering and the L-curve, showing why inversion amplifies noise and how regularisation filters it.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Blurring is easy, deblurring is not</h2>
    <p>A camera out of focus, a thermometer with a slow response or heat spreading through a wall all act as a <em>blur</em>: each measurement is a weighted average of the unknown signal around it. On a grid this is a matrix product, <em>g = A f + ε</em>. Computing <em>g</em> from <em>f</em> is stable. Going back is where the trouble starts. The singular value decomposition (SVD), which writes any matrix as a sum of independent components, each with its own gain σ<sub>i</sub>, shows why.</p>
    <Equation tex={String.raw`A = U \Sigma V^{\mathsf T} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sigma_i\, u_i v_i^{\mathsf T}, \qquad \sigma_1 \ge \sigma_2 \ge \dots \ge \sigma_n \ge 0 .`} />
    <p>The right singular vectors <em>v<sub>i</sub></em> are patterns in the signal (smooth for small <em>i</em>, oscillating for large <em>i</em>); the operator multiplies each one by <em>σ<sub>i</sub></em> and maps it to the pattern <em>u<sub>i</sub></em> in the data. A blur damps oscillating patterns strongly, so the singular values fall off by many orders of magnitude.</p>
    <h2>The naive inverse divides by tiny numbers</h2>
    <p>Inverting the decomposition gives the solution as a sum over the same patterns:</p>
    <Equation tex={String.raw`f^{\dagger} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{u_i^{\mathsf T} g}{\sigma_i}\, v_i = f + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{u_i^{\mathsf T} \varepsilon}{\sigma_i}\, v_i .`} />
    <p>The noise term <em>u<sub>i</sub><sup>T</sup>ε</em> has roughly the same size for every <em>i</em>, but it is divided by <em>σ<sub>i</sub></em>. For the last components that means multiplying noise by 10<sup>10</sup> or more. The <em>Picard plot</em> shows the competition: as long as the data coefficients |<em>u<sub>i</sub><sup>T</sup>g</em>| decay faster than the singular values, the signal can be recovered; where they level off at the noise floor, the ratio explodes.</p>
  </div>

  <SvdExplorer />

  <div class="try">
    <h3>Exercises</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Set the noise to 10⁻⁸ and slide <em>k</em> from 1 to 64. Where do the data coefficients (amber) stop following the singular values and flatten out? Compare that index with the value of <em>k</em> that gives the smallest error.
        <Answer lang="en"><p>At this noise level the data coefficients follow the singular values down to roughly <em>i</em> ≈ 45–55 and then level off at the noise floor. The smallest error (about 8 %) is reached near <em>k</em> ≈ 55, close to where they flatten. Keeping components beyond that point adds terms that are mostly noise divided by tiny singular values, and the error explodes.</p></Answer></li>
      <li>Raise the noise to 10⁻². How many components can you now keep? Why does more noise mean a smoother (less detailed) reconstruction?
        <Answer lang="en"><p>At 10⁻² the best choice drops to about <em>k</em> ≈ 24 and the error rises to about 23 %. A higher noise floor hides more of the data coefficients, so fewer components can be trusted. The discarded components are the oscillating ones, which carry the fine detail, so the reconstruction becomes smoother.</p></Answer></li>
      <li>Increase the blur width. The singular values fall faster. What does that do to the sharp edges of the box and to the narrow bump?
        <Answer lang="en"><p>With a blur width of 0.06 the condition number reaches about 10¹⁸ and only about 19 components can be kept (error about 26 %): the box edges are rounded and the narrow bump is flattened, because the components that describe them are lost in the noise. With a narrow blur (0.015) all 64 components survive and the error falls to about 2 %.</p></Answer></li>
      <li>Switch to Tikhonov and move λ. Watch the violet point travel along the L-curve. Which part of the curve corresponds to over-smoothing, and which to noise amplification? Is the corner a good choice?
        <Answer lang="en"><p>Small λ puts the point on the vertical branch: the residual cannot fall below the noise, while the solution norm grows enormously (noise amplification). Large λ puts it on the horizontal branch: the solution is small and smooth but no longer fits the data (over-smoothing). The corner balances the two and usually lands close to the best λ, but it is a heuristic; the discrepancy principle is an alternative when the noise level is known.</p></Answer></li>
      <li>Tikhonov does not cut components off; it damps them smoothly with the filter factor below. Compare it with truncation at <em>k</em> such that σ<sub>k</sub> ≈ λ.
        <Answer lang="en"><p>The filter factor σ²/(σ² + λ²) is close to 1 when σ ≫ λ and close to 0 when σ ≪ λ, so Tikhonov behaves like truncation at the index where σ<sub>k</sub> ≈ λ, with a smooth transition instead of a sharp cut. At noise 10⁻³ the best Tikhonov λ (about 0.008) corresponds to σ<sub>k</sub> ≈ λ at <em>k</em> ≈ 29, and the best truncation is <em>k</em> = 30: the errors are almost equal (about 17 %).</p></Answer></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Regularisation is filtering</h2>
    <p>Both methods keep the well-determined components and suppress the rest. Truncated SVD uses a sharp cut; Tikhonov, which minimises ‖<em>Af − g</em>‖² + λ²‖<em>f</em>‖², uses smooth filter factors:</p>
    <Equation tex={String.raw`f_\lambda = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \varphi_i\, \frac{u_i^{\mathsf T} g}{\sigma_i}\, v_i, \qquad \varphi_i^{\mathrm{TSVD}} = \begin{cases} 1 & i \le k \\ 0 & i > k \end{cases}, \qquad \varphi_i^{\mathrm{Tik}} = \frac{\sigma_i^2}{\sigma_i^2 + \lambda^2} .`} />
    <p>The <em>L-curve</em> plots the size of the solution against the size of the residual for every λ. Too little regularisation gives a huge, oscillating solution that fits the noise (the vertical branch); too much gives a small, over-smoothed solution that does not fit the data (the horizontal branch). The corner is a common, if not infallible, compromise. Another rule, the discrepancy principle, picks λ so that the residual equals the expected noise level, printed in the readout.</p>
    <p>The same analysis explains the <a href={url('/learn/inverse-problems/abel-transform/')}>Abel transform module</a>, where the operator is a projection instead of a blur, and it is the reason why physics-informed networks need a well-posed loss (see <a href={url('/learn/machine-learning/physics-informed-learning/')}>the physics-informed learning module</a>).</p>
  </div>
</LearnModule>
